---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import CalorieCalculator from '@/components/calculators/CalorieCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How many calories should I eat a day?',
    a: 'It depends on your age, sex, size and how active you are. As a rough guide, many adult women need around 1,800–2,400 kcal and men around 2,200–3,000 kcal to maintain their weight. The calculator estimates your own figure.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How accurate is this calorie calculator?',
    a: 'It uses the Mifflin–St Jeor equation, which studies have found to be the most accurate of the common formulas for most adults, typically within about 10% of measured values. Treat the result as a starting point and adjust it based on how your weight changes over a few weeks.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many calories do I need to cut to lose weight?',
    a: 'A kilogram of body fat stores about 7,700 kcal (about 3,500 kcal per pound). Eating 550 kcal a day less than you burn gives a loss of about 0.5 kg (1 lb) a week. Most guidance suggests losing no more than 0.5–1 kg (1–2 lb) a week.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?',
    a: 'BMR (basal metabolic rate) is the energy your body uses at complete rest, just to keep you alive. TDEE (total daily energy expenditure) adds everything you do in a day, from walking to exercise. TDEE is BMR multiplied by an activity factor.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which activity level should I choose?',
    a: 'Most people overestimate. If you have a desk job and exercise two or three times a week, “Lightly active” is usually closer than “Moderately active”. If you’re not losing or gaining as expected, try the next level down or up.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="calorie-calculator"
  title="Calorie Calculator – Daily Calories to Lose or Gain Weight"
  description="Free calorie calculator. Estimate the calories you need each day to maintain, lose or gain weight, based on your age, sex, height, weight and activity level."
  intro="Estimate how many calories you burn each day and how many to eat to maintain, lose or gain weight, based on your age, sex, size and how active you are."
  faqs={faqs}
  updated="2026-09-27"
>
  <CalorieCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="learn">
    <h2>How to use the calorie calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Enter your sex, age, height and weight.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Choose your activity level</strong> honestly; it has the biggest effect on the result.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>You’ll see the calories to maintain your weight, plus daily targets for gentle, steady or faster weight loss or gain.</p>

    <h2>How the calculation works</h2>
    <p>First, your basal metabolic rate (BMR) is estimated with the <strong>Mifflin–St Jeor equation</strong>:</p>
    <div class="formula">Men: BMR = 10 × weight (kg) + 6.25 × height (cm) − 5 × age + 5</div>
    <div class="formula">Women: BMR = 10 × weight (kg) + 6.25 × height (cm) − 5 × age − 161</div>
    <p>Then it’s multiplied by an activity factor to get your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE):</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Activity level</th><th>Factor</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sedentary: little or no exercise</td><td>1.2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lightly active: 1–3 days a week</td><td>1.375</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Moderately active: 3–5 days a week</td><td>1.55</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Very active: 6–7 days a week</td><td>1.725</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Extra active: physical job or training twice a day</td><td>1.9</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>30-year-old man</strong>, <strong>80 kg</strong> and <strong>180 cm</strong>, who exercises 3–5 days a week:
    </p>
    <div class="formula">BMR = 10 × 80 + 6.25 × 180 − 5 × 30 + 5 = 1,780 kcal</div>
    <div class="formula">TDEE = 1,780 × 1.55 ≈ 2,759 kcal a day</div>
    <p>
      To lose about <strong>0.5 kg a week</strong>, he would eat about 550 kcal less: roughly <strong>2,209 kcal a day</strong> (0.5 kg × 7,700 kcal ÷ 7 days = 550 kcal).
    </p>

    <h2>Tips for using your calorie target</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Track for two to three weeks,</strong> then adjust: if your weight isn’t changing as expected, move your target by 100–200 kcal.</li>
      <li><strong>Recalculate as your weight changes,</strong> since a smaller body burns fewer calories.</li>
      <li><strong>Prioritise protein, fibre and whole foods</strong> to stay full on fewer calories.</li>
      <li><strong>Don’t go too low:</strong> very low-calorie diets should only be followed under medical supervision.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Want to check whether your weight is in a healthy range first? Use the <a href="/bmi-calculator">BMI calculator</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
